The microphone port, mono like the factory does it

Indispensable companion to 0044. Without this one, 0044 cannot test what it
sets out to test.

## What this phone's factory Android says

Taken from its `mixer_paths_wcd9375qrd.xml` (see `docs/STOCK-REFERENCE.md`),
counting every microphone path:

    TX_CDC_DMA_TX_3 Channels = "One"     9 paths   <- one microphone
    TX_CDC_DMA_TX_3 Channels = "Two"     3 paths   <- dual microphone (endfire)
    TX_CDC_DMA_TX_3 Channels = "Three"   2 paths
    TX_CDC_DMA_TX_3 Channels = "Four"    1 path

So that control is, literally, **how many microphones the port carries**, and
the factory call path (`handset-mic` -> `dmic3`) sets it to **one**.

## What we do

`sm8250_be_hw_params_fixup()` forces **every** backend to 2 channels, without
distinguishing input from output. So the microphone port runs at 2 channels
always. Measured by recording 3 seconds:

    channel 0:  max=32768  rms=1457    <- the microphone
    channel 1:  max=0      rms=0       <- exact digital silence

The second channel does not exist in the device: our own fixup invents it.

## Why without this 0044 is worthless

0044 makes it possible to declare a single microphone to the DSP. But if the
port keeps delivering two interleaved channels and we tell it there is one, the
vocproc will read the frame with the wrong layout. The factory configuration is
**mono in both places**, and to be able to test it the port has to be settable
to mono.

## What it does

A module parameter, adjustable on the fly:

    /sys/module/snd_soc_sm8250/parameters/tx_be_channels

It only affects `TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3`. **The default stays at 2**, so as not to
touch normal recording, which works today and is the one thing I do not want to
break. The `audio/test-tx-channels` sweep lowers it to 1 in the phases that need it.

--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c
@@ -18,6 +18,31 @@
 
 #define MI2S_BCLK_RATE		1536000
 
+/*
+ * Codec capture port channels, adjustable at runtime:
+ *
+ *   /sys/module/snd_soc_sm8250/parameters/tx_be_channels
+ *
+ * sm8250_be_hw_params_fixup() forces ALL backends to 2 channels. For
+ * playback that is fine, but not for the microphone: this device has ONE
+ * microphone in the voice path, and the second channel of the port is silence
+ * exact digital zero (measured: max=0 over 3 seconds of recording).
+ *
+ * The factory Android of this same phone sets it to one. In its
+ * mixer_paths_wcd9375qrd.xml, ALL the paths of a single microphone carry:
+ *
+ *     <ctl name="TX_CDC_DMA_TX_3 Channels" value="One" />
+ *
+ * and only the dual microphone (endfire) ones raise it to "Two". So that
+ * control is the number of microphones of the port, and from the factory voice is mono.
+ *
+ * It is left at 2 by default so as not to disturb normal recording, which today
+ * works. The sweep lowers it to 1 to test the factory configuration.
+ */
+static unsigned int tx_be_channels = 2;
+module_param(tx_be_channels, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(tx_be_channels, "channels of the TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3 port (1-8)");
+
 struct sm8250_snd_data {
 	bool stream_prepared[AFE_PORT_MAX];
 	struct snd_soc_card *card;
@@ -63,9 +88,16 @@
 	struct snd_interval *channels = hw_param_interval(params,
 					SNDRV_PCM_HW_PARAM_CHANNELS);
 	struct snd_mask *fmt = hw_param_mask(params, SNDRV_PCM_HW_PARAM_FORMAT);
+	struct snd_soc_dai *cpu_dai = snd_soc_rtd_to_cpu(rtd, 0);
 
 	rate->min = rate->max = 48000;
 	channels->min = channels->max = 2;
+
+	/* The microphone port, mono as from the factory. See tx_be_channels. */
+	if (cpu_dai->id == TX_CODEC_DMA_TX_3 && tx_be_channels >= 1 &&
+	    tx_be_channels <= 8)
+		channels->min = channels->max = tx_be_channels;
+
 	snd_mask_set_format(fmt, SNDRV_PCM_FORMAT_S16_LE);
 
 	return 0;
